# Cursor Cloud-specific instructions

Read `AGENTS.md` first and treat it as authoritative. These notes only cover the pre-provisioned Cursor Cloud environment and must not override the repository's safety, testing, or architecture rules.

## Toolchain

The image provides Node 24 and the global `vp` (Vite+) CLI with its bundled pnpm. Use `vp` as the package manager and task runner.

- The base image may expose an older Node from `/exec-daemon` in non-login shells. Use a login shell or ensure the Node 24 installation wins on `PATH`; the repository requires the version declared in `package.json`.
- Run `vp i` if worktree setup did not install dependencies.
- Use focused formatter, lint, typecheck, and test commands for touched files and packages. Do not run repository-wide checks unless the developer asks.

## Running the web app

- Start the server and web client from the repository root with `vp run dev`. Read the actual ports and runtime home from the `[dev-runner]` output because occupied ports may shift them.
- Worktree development state belongs in the worktree's ignored `.t3` directory. Never start a server against or write to the live `~/.t3/userdata` database.
- Dev is single-origin. Never set `VITE_HTTP_URL` or `VITE_WS_URL`; Vite proxies backend routes for local and remote clients.
- The web app requires pairing. Use the complete one-time pairing URL once. Treat its token as a secret; send a fresh URL only for an explicit human handoff, and never commit or capture it in screenshots.
- Use the `test-pylon-app` skill for retained browser sessions, shared environments, pairing recovery, or SQLite fixtures.

Provider CLIs (`codex`, `claude`, `cursor-agent`, `opencode`, and others supported by the server) are probed from `PATH`. Restart the dev server only when a newly installed provider must be discovered immediately, and stop only a process whose PID belongs to the current task.

## Mobile and native work

- Use `test-pylon-mobile` for device selection, isolated backend setup, pairing, Metro reuse, and focused verification.
- Cursor Cloud has no hardware virtualization, so do not expect an Android Emulator to run. Use a physical device or the repository's EAS workflow when execution is required.
- Inspect `apps/mobile/README.md` and the current Expo/native configuration before generating projects or building. Do not preserve old environment notes as permanent blockers without reproducing them against the current source.
- For iOS work on a macOS host, use the repository's `ios-debugger-agent` and `ios-simulator-browser` skills with the pinned XcodeBuildMCP configuration.
